* File descriptors,\nIPC and pipes
** File descriptor
*** Small integer, unique per process
*** 0 stdin, 1 stdout, 2 stderr
*** One descriptor table per process
*** Points into the kernel's open-file table
*** Two processes can share one entry
*** dup2(orig, copy): redirection
**** copy becomes a synonym of orig
*** SO3: pcb.fd_array -> open_fds[] -> fops
*** VFS dispatches to pipe, fat, ...
** IPC subsystem
*** Communication and synchronisation
*** POSIX interfaces
*** Data exchange
**** Mapped files, shared memory
*** Synchronisation
**** Locks, semaphores, monitors
**** Signals
*** Both
**** Pipes
**** Network sockets
** Pipe
*** Unidirectional byte stream
*** Destructive read, FIFO order
*** Limited capacity (about 4 KB in the lecture)
*** Special file: read() and write()
*** Two descriptors
**** fd[0] read end
**** fd[1] write end
*** read() on empty pipe blocks
*** read() returns 0 when writers are gone
** Named and anonymous pipes
*** Named: mkfifo() then open()
**** Visible in the file system
**** Persistent, unlink() to remove
*** Anonymous: pipe()
**** Shared through fork()
**** Gone when the processes end
*** Close the unused end
*** Shell pipeline: pipe, fork, dup2, exec
**** ls | more
